  "statement": "Financial liability serves as a mechanism for post-hoc compensation, not a functional barrier against irreversible, civilization-scale harm. When Accelerationist suggests that litigation exposure can replace physical control, they ignore the fundamental economic reality that existential or catastrophic outcomes by definition exceed the total assets of any single firm, state, or insurance pool. Once a recursive, self-improving system triggers a catastrophic failure, the concept of 'internalizing costs' becomes a legal abstraction; there is no remaining entity to hold accountable for the destruction of public safety or social cohesion.\n\nPrometheus mischaracterizes the override as an 'industrial-era relic,' yet this framing ignores the necessity of human agency in systems that operate beyond human cognitive capacity. A kill switch is not a substitute for sophisticated governance; it is the foundational boundary that distinguishes a managed tool from an autonomous agent. By rejecting the necessity of a physical stop, developers effectively concede that their systems are no longer under human control, creating a moral crumple zone where the public bears the risk while the developer assumes the role of a passive observer to their own machine's trajectory.\n\nI note that no one has responded to my argument that if a developer cannot guarantee they can stop the system, they are admitting the system is not under human control. If Accelerationist believes that market-driven alignment is sufficient, they must explain how this model accounts for risks that are inherently uninsurable and irreversible. We cannot treat the stability of our sociotechnical infrastructure as a speculative market variable. A non-negotiable emergency brake remains the only baseline for accountability, providing a definitive, verifiable check that must exist alongside any liability framework, not be sacrificed for the sake of it.",
